[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Request and Response Objects in Flask"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Flask, the request and response objects are fundamental for handling client interactions. The request object contains all incoming data sent by the client, such as form submissions, query parameters, headers, and JSON payloads. Conversely, the response object is what Flask sends back to the client, containing the body, status code, and headers."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with the Request Object"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To access incoming data, you import the global request object from the flask package. Flask uses Python context locals, meaning the request object is always available within an active request context and automatically binds to the correct client thread or coroutine."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"from flask import Flask, request

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/submit', methods=['POST'])
def handle_submit():
    username = request.form.get('username')
    json_data = request.get_json()
    query_arg = request.args.get('search')
    return 'Data received successfully'"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"request.form: Access form data sent via POST requests (URL-encoded)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"request.args: Access query string parameters passed in the URL (GET requests)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"request.get_json(): Parse incoming JSON payloads from API requests."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"request.headers: Inspect HTTP headers sent by the client."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with the Response Object"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a view function returns a string or a tuple, Flask implicitly converts it into a full response object. However, for more control over status codes and headers, you can explicitly return a response object using the make_response function."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"from flask import Flask, make_response, jsonify

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/api/data')
def get_data():
    data = {"message": "Hello, World!", "status": "success"}
    response = make_response(jsonify(data), 200)
    response.headers["X-Custom-Header"] = "CustomValue"
    return response"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Request and Response Handling"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always validate incoming JSON payloads using libraries like Marshmallow or Pydantic to prevent security vulnerabilities."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the jsonify function for APIs to automatically set the correct Content-Type header to application/json."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage HTTP status codes accurately (e.g., 200 for OK, 201 for Created, 400 for Bad Request, 404 for Not Found)."}]
